but at least now there are plenty of  differences too  when British explorer Captain James Cook  first reached Hawaii in 1778 the  population was around 300,000 and they  all spoke Hawaiian American missionaries  soon arrived as well as American  businessmen who established sugarcane  plantations along with them came  diseases such as smallpox which wiped  out much of the native population by  1860 there were only around 60,000  Native Hawaiians and by 1920 only 24,000  many immigrants also came to Hawaii to  work on plantations during this period  from Asian countries like Japan China  and Philippines speaking their  respective languages in 1893 the kingdom  of Hawaii was overthrown with the  intention of annexing it to the United  States in 1896 a law was passed making  English the language of instruction in  Hawaiian schools Hawaiian was not made  illegal to speak but at school children  were disciplined if they spoke Hawaiian  this along with the largely decimated  native population put the Hawaiian  language at serious risk of dying out  over the next century it's a language  that many predicted would be dead by now  but in recent decades there's been more  attention to preserving and promoting  the language in the 1980s new Hawaiian  immersion schools began to be  established including immersion  preschools in 1979 before the  establishment of these schools there  were only around 800 native speakers of  Hawaiian remaining but now according to  the latest u.s. census there were around  18,000 people who speak Hawaiian at home  that doesn't necessarily mean it's their  first language but that they are fluent  speakers and many of them must be among  the people who studied at immersion  schools now let's take a look at some of  the languages key features pronunciation  and orthography Hawaiian is written with  only 8 consonant letters one is a  glottal stop called Akina we heard it  before in Hawaii again notice that  Hawaii also note that certain letters  can represent two different sounds  depending on the dialect or accent but  spelling is now standardized to these  particular letters the letter W can  represent WA or it's more commonly  pronounced these days but it depends on  the speaker and the dialect there  beaches influenced by it as well as on  the surrounding sounds Hawaiian has five  vowels ah eh e o+ kaha Cole a lion  placed over a vowel to indicate that  it's a long vowel there are no consonant  clusters in Hawaiian in other words  there are never two consonants in a row  every syllable ends in a vowel Aloha  Mahalo Ohana etc the stress of a word  usually falls on the penultimate  syllable the second last syllable  here are some basic phrases in Hawaiian  we've seen the first one already Aloha  hello goodbye or love aloha kakahiaka  good morning between the hours of about  6 a.m. to 10:00 a.m.  aloha baqia good day from around 10 a.m.  to 2 p.m.  Aloha Hui Nala good afternoon from  around 2 p.m. to 6 p.m.  Aloha hee hee good evening from around 6  p.m. to 10 p.m. be here oi how are you  make a you I'm good  Waco Inoue what's your name ok Norco we  know my name is Kaia Noah  notice here that Co your and ku my are  two completely different words so you  can't skip the glottal stop in Hawaiian  words all Oh Lou please Mahalo  thank you hey Mikey you're welcome  literally a little thing let's examine  the word order of Hawaiian a little bit  look at the sentence make a you it  doesn't have a verb but notice that the  predicate comes first and the subject  comes second word for word it's good I  this is very common in Austronesian  languages and it immediately reminded me  of Tagalog a language of the Philippines  okay Noah Cowan or word for word it's  personal topic marker ke Noah my name  the subject is Kahlua Noah and the  predicate is okay Noah also it's similar  if the predicate is an adjective  nanika Vahini the woman is beautiful  literally beautiful the woman that's a  full sentence kavaja Nannini means the  beautiful woman and is not a full  sentence there's no predicate it's  simply a noun phrase in Hawaiian noun  phrases if there's an adjective the  comes after the noun whereas in English  it comes before it another example  NUI Kahala means the house is big but  Kahala Nui means the big house if we had  a verb we can see that Hawaiian is VSO  Aloha oh yeah oh I love you word for  word love I you and you can see that  it's vs Oh verb subject object as a side  note ow is the first person singular  subject pronoun yah Oh a is the second  person singular object pronoun  Makemake Oh Evie I want some water  word-for-word want I direct object  marker the water again we see that the  verb comes first followed by the subject  then by the object this word II is a  direct object marker that is used before  common nouns so now we have an idea of  the most basic word order of Hawaiian  articles and determiners we've seen many  nouns preceded by car this is a definite  article there's one more ke ke is used  before nouns beginning with ke e a or o  like bikumu the teacher before nouns  starting with any other letter it's cop  which is more common there's also a  plural definite article na na Kumu that  teachers there's also an indefinite  article hey hey kuma a teacher you can  make the indefinite noun plural by  adding a plural marker mu hey mo Kumu  teachers  similarly there are demonstratives k ya  means this K yo Popo ki this cat k na  means that for things that are near the  listener can Popo key that cat kala  means that for things that are far from  both the speaker and the listener kala  Popo key to make the demonstrative  scleral you can add mo k mo Popo key  these cats  now let's look at possessives oka Evo  Killa that's my dog  over there word for word it's equate of  sentence marker my dog that in the  distance just a quick note about oh this  word comes at the beginning of equative  sentences which are used to specifically  identify something but back to the main  point here this word here is a  possessive meaning mai kakou  possessives in Hawaiian are interesting  because there's more than one version of  each one  kau is an a class possessive a class  possessives are used for things you can  choose whether to possess or not if you  have a dog you probably chose to buy it  and can choose to get rid of it of  course you probably won't because dogs  are our man's best friend but you know  you could the OU class equivalent is  Coco Oh class possessives are mainly  used with nouns that you cannot  choose to possess you inherently possess  them or kukaku he Nakia this is my  sister you can't decide whether you have  a sister or not so this is Oh class  there is an exception for this for  family members below you in your family  tree like children and grandchildren a  class is used a class can be used for  things that you create and I guess you  create your children making them a class  things like body parts are also Oh class  Komaki means my eye : ma means my arm or  my hand etc one caveat is that clothing  and structures that you can enter or  ride are also Oh class as though they  are extensions of your body  okoma Kohala kala that is our house over  there note that with Hawaiian  possessives and Hawaiian personal  pronouns there is first person plural  inclusive in other words we or our  includes the listener and first person  plural exclusive in other words we or  our doesn't include the listener this is  common in Austronesian languages I've  seen something similar in  malay/indonesian and Tagalog this  possessive here kohaku excludes the  listener one more thing which is special  about Hawaiian possessives and personal  pronouns is that there is not just one  set of plural forms there are dual forms  which refer to pairs of people and there  are plural forms which refer to groups  of three or more new Ecola khojali their  house is big this refers to three or  more people's house but if I said Nui  Cola holla it would refer to two  people's house now let's look a little  more closely at verbs you saw before  that there's no verb meaning to be  another important thing to point out is  that verbs are not conjugated for person  or number they stay the same regardless  of the subject he min you I sing  he Minnie oi you sing he Minnie oh yeah  he sings or she sings he Minnie la ku  they sing and so on  tense aspect and mood markers are used  before or after the verb to indicate the  relationship of action to time for  example k + verb + n-- a equals the  present continuous Gahanna neo I am  working a plus verb equals the  perfective aspect showing that an action  is complete often this translates as the  past tense waha know I worked or I have  worked a + verb plus an ax equals the  imperfective aspect which indicates that  an action has not been completed this  often translates as the future tense in  English but can also be used for an  incomplete action in the past  Ahana now I will work or I was working a  plus the verb equals the imperative form  Ahana work it can also function as the  infinitive to work for the negative  imperative you use my Mae Hana don't  work now let's look at a final sentence  in Hawaiian and break it down well hello  a Coue Mirko Mahalo Mahalo Poulet I went  shopping with my friends on Sunday  word-for-word it's perfective marker go  i infinitive shop with my plural marker  friend on the sunday first we see the  perfective aspect marker which shows  that this action is complete again  notice that the sentence is verb initial  the subject comes after the verb next we  see the infinitive form of the verb  meaning to buy or shop next May is a  preposition meaning with there are a few  prepositions in Hawaiian e meaning to on  it or aunt ma meaning on it or at but  not to Mae meaning with oh and ah  meaning of there are two forms here  because there's a distinction between Oh  class and a class and no and not meaning  belonging to again  the first one is Oh class 